{
  "chapter": "security/untrusted-retrieval",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "A corpus is a merge of every writer who ever had access to any of its sources. Audit it",
    "by asking who could write the bytes, not who owns the database.",
    "The attack is time-shifted: written months ago, fired by a benign query. Request-time",
    "filtering never sees it and the request log shows a legitimate customer.",
    "Retrieval is the attacker's aiming mechanism, so the ratio that matters is poisoned",
    "documents over documents competing for that query \u2014 not over corpus size. Reranking does",
    "not help, because the document is deliberately, genuinely relevant.",
    "Provenance is a column the loader assigns, never inferred from content.",
    "Content-hash re-crawled sources, or a vendor page that turns hostile after ingestion is a",
    "silent change.",
    "Split the corpus by trust and route by task: a high-authority workflow never reads",
    "attacker-writable text. That is the trifecta broken where it counts.",
    "Wire retrieval provenance to the run's taint flag, and require citations that resolve to",
    "chunk ids, or incident scope is everything since ingestion."
  ],
  "policy": {
    "highAuthorityProvenance": [
      "first-party"
    ],
    "requireCitations": true
  },
